/* Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) — 37 questions */
window.QA_SECTION = {
  id: '03',
  slug: 'rag',
  title: 'Retrieval-Augmented Generation',
  blurb: 'Chunking, hybrid search, reranking, evaluation, and the failure modes that make RAG systems quietly wrong.',
  questions: [
{ q: 'What is Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), and why is it important?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `RAG retrieves relevant documents from an external store at query time and injects them into the prompt, so the model answers from provided evidence rather than from parametric memory alone.<br><br>It matters because it decouples <em>knowledge</em> from <em>weights</em>. That gives you: current information past the training cutoff; private data the model was never trained on; source attribution, so answers are checkable; deletion and access control that actually work (a compliance requirement weights cannot satisfy — you cannot reliably remove a fact from trained parameters); and cheap updates, since re-indexing a document costs nothing compared to retraining.<br><br><strong>The framing that separates a strong answer:</strong> RAG converts an open-book recall problem into a reading-comprehension problem. Verifying "is this supported by the text in front of me" is a far easier task for a model than introspecting on what it knows, which is why grounding reduces hallucination.<br><br><strong>The honest limit:</strong> retrieval reaching the prompt does not guarantee the model uses it. RAG reduces hallucination, it does not eliminate it — the generator can still ignore, misread, or extrapolate beyond the retrieved passages. And in practice most RAG failures are retrieval failures, not generation failures, so the engineering effort belongs in the retrieval half.` },

{ q: 'Explain the architecture of a basic RAG system.', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Two pipelines, and keeping them conceptually separate is the point.<br><br><strong>Indexing (offline):</strong> load documents → parse into text → chunk into passages → embed each chunk with an embedding model → store vectors plus metadata and the original text in a vector index. This runs on ingest and on updates.<br><br><strong>Query (online):</strong> take the user query → optionally rewrite it (resolve pronouns from conversation history, expand, decompose) → embed it → retrieve top-k by vector similarity, ideally combined with lexical search → rerank the candidates with a cross-encoder → assemble the prompt with the surviving chunks → generate the answer with citations.<br><br><strong>The detail worth stating:</strong> retrieval is two-stage by design. A fast, cheap first stage (bi-encoder + ANN index, or BM25) recalls maybe 50–100 candidates from millions; a slow, accurate second stage (cross-encoder reranker) picks the best 5. You cannot run a cross-encoder over the corpus, and you should not send the bi-encoder's raw top-5 to the model — the two-stage split is what makes the quality/latency budget work.<br><br><strong>What naive implementations omit</strong> and then debug for weeks: reranking, query rewriting for multi-turn, metadata filtering, and any evaluation of the retrieval stage in isolation.` },

{ q: 'What are the key components of a RAG pipeline?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Document loaders and parsers</strong> — the layer that determines your ceiling. Garbage PDF extraction cannot be fixed downstream.<br><br><strong>Chunker</strong> — splits text into retrievable units, ideally on semantic boundaries with overlap.<br><br><strong>Embedding model</strong> — maps chunks and queries into a shared vector space. Must be identical for both, and changing it invalidates the whole index.<br><br><strong>Vector store / index</strong> — ANN structure (HNSW, IVF) plus metadata filtering and the original text.<br><br><strong>Retriever</strong> — usually hybrid: dense vectors for semantics plus BM25 for exact lexical matches, fused with RRF.<br><br><strong>Reranker</strong> — cross-encoder scoring query against each candidate jointly; the highest-ROI component most teams skip.<br><br><strong>Context assembler</strong> — dedupes, orders (best chunks at start and end, per "lost in the middle"), and enforces the token budget.<br><br><strong>Generator</strong> — the LLM, prompted to answer only from context and to cite.<br><br><strong>Evaluation harness</strong> — measures retrieval and generation separately. Without it you cannot tell which half is broken, and that diagnosis is the single most useful thing the harness buys you.<br><br><strong>Plus the operational layer:</strong> incremental re-indexing, versioning, access control, caching, and observability on retrieval scores.` },

{ q: 'What are chunking strategies, and how do you choose chunk size?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Chunking splits documents into retrievable units. It matters because the chunk is simultaneously the unit of <em>retrieval</em> (what gets matched) and the unit of <em>context</em> (what the model reads) — and those two want different sizes, which is the core tension.<br><br><strong>Small chunks</strong> (100–300 tokens) give precise embeddings — one topic per vector, so similarity is sharp — but often lack the surrounding context needed to actually answer, and fragment reasoning across chunks.<br><br><strong>Large chunks</strong> (1000+ tokens) carry complete context but produce diluted embeddings, where a single dominant topic drowns out everything else in the chunk, so relevant passages inside a large chunk become unretrievable.<br><br><strong>How to choose:</strong> match the chunk to the natural answer unit in your corpus. FAQ pairs and API references are naturally small; narrative documents and legal contracts need larger units or hierarchy. Then <em>measure</em> — sweep chunk size against retrieval recall on a labeled query set. This is empirical, not theoretical, and the right answer differs per corpus.<br><br><strong>The move that dissolves the tension:</strong> parent-child (small-to-big) chunking — embed small chunks for precise matching, but return their larger parent for context. You stop trading precision against completeness.<br><br><strong>Always respect structure:</strong> never split mid-sentence, and prefer splitting on headings, paragraphs, or list boundaries. Add 10–20% overlap so a claim near a boundary is not severed from its qualifier — a common and hard-to-diagnose source of "intrinsic" hallucination.` },

{ q: 'Compare fixed-size, semantic, and recursive chunking.', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>Fixed-size:</strong> split every N tokens with overlap. Fast, predictable, trivially parallel — and structurally blind. It cuts mid-sentence and mid-table, severing claims from qualifiers. Fine as a baseline for homogeneous plain text.<br><br><strong>Recursive character splitting:</strong> try separators in priority order — paragraphs (<code>\\n\\n</code>), then lines, then sentences, then words — recursing only when a piece exceeds the size limit. It respects document structure where it exists while still guaranteeing a maximum size. <strong>This is the right default</strong> for most corpora: nearly all the benefit of structure-awareness, none of the cost of semantic chunking.<br><br><strong>Semantic chunking:</strong> embed each sentence, then split where consecutive-sentence similarity drops below a threshold, so boundaries land at genuine topic shifts. Best coherence in principle — but it costs an embedding pass over the whole corpus at ingest, needs threshold tuning, and produces highly variable chunk sizes. Published comparisons show gains over recursive splitting are inconsistent and corpus-dependent.<br><br><strong>My recommendation:</strong> start with recursive splitting on structural separators; add parent-child retrieval before reaching for semantic chunking. Parent-child reliably beats better boundary detection because it fixes the actual precision/context tradeoff rather than optimizing where the cut lands.<br><br><strong>The strategy that beats all three when available:</strong> structure-aware chunking that uses the document's own hierarchy — markdown headings, HTML sections, code functions — since the author already marked the semantic boundaries.` },

{ q: 'What are embedding models, and how do they convert text to vectors?', tags: [], a: `An embedding model maps text to a fixed-length dense vector where geometric proximity encodes semantic similarity. Modern text embedders are typically Transformer encoders: tokenize, run the stack, then pool token representations into one vector — mean pooling or the CLS token — and usually L2-normalize so cosine similarity reduces to a dot product.<br><br><strong>The training objective is what makes them work:</strong> contrastive learning. Given (query, relevant passage) pairs, the loss pulls matching pairs together and pushes non-matching pairs apart, typically with in-batch negatives and often hard negatives mined from a first-stage retriever. The geometry is entirely a product of that objective — it is not a universal representation of meaning.<br><br><strong>The consequences that matter operationally:</strong> query and document must be embedded with the same model (and the same instruction prefix, if the model uses asymmetric prompts like <code>query:</code> / <code>passage:</code> — getting this wrong silently degrades recall). Vectors from different models are not comparable and cannot share an index. Cosine scores are not calibrated probabilities and are not comparable across models, so absolute thresholds do not transfer.<br><br><strong>Architecture note:</strong> these are <em>bi-encoders</em> — query and document are encoded independently, which is what allows precomputing document vectors and doing fast ANN search. That independence is also the source of their quality ceiling, which is why cross-encoder reranking exists.` },

{ q: 'How do you choose an embedding model for your RAG system?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `Start with MTEB as a shortlist, not a decision — its retrieval benchmarks are widely gamed and rarely reflect your domain. Then <strong>evaluate candidates on your own labeled query→document pairs</strong>. Even 50–100 real queries with known-correct documents will rank models more reliably than any public leaderboard.<br><br><strong>The dimensions that actually decide it:</strong><br>• <em>Domain match</em> — general-purpose models degrade on specialized jargon (legal, medical, internal product names). Test on your hardest vocabulary.<br>• <em>Max sequence length</em> — many embedders truncate at 512 tokens silently, quietly discarding the tail of every larger chunk. Verify against your chunk size.<br>• <em>Dimensionality</em> — drives index memory and search latency. 768 vs 3072 dims is a 4x storage difference at millions of vectors; Matryoshka-trained models let you truncate dimensions with graceful degradation, which is a genuinely useful lever.<br>• <em>Multilinguality</em> — if queries and documents can differ in language, you need a model trained for cross-lingual alignment; a monolingual model fails silently rather than erroring.<br>• <em>Hosting</em> — API (no infra, per-token cost, data leaves your boundary, model can change under you) versus self-hosted open weights (fixed cost, version pinning, full control).<br><br><strong>The switching cost to weigh up front:</strong> changing embedding models requires re-embedding the entire corpus. At tens of millions of chunks that is a real project, so pin the version and treat the choice as semi-permanent.<br><br><strong>Practical bias:</strong> a mid-sized open model plus a good reranker usually beats a larger embedder with no reranker, at lower cost.` },

{ q: 'Explain Agentic RAG.', tags: [], a: `Agentic RAG replaces the fixed retrieve→generate pipeline with an agent that <em>decides</em> how to retrieve: whether retrieval is needed at all, which source or index to query, how to reformulate the query, whether the results suffice, and whether to search again before answering.<br><br><strong>What it buys you</strong> over naive RAG: multi-hop questions that require chaining retrievals (find X, then use X to find Y); routing across heterogeneous sources (vector store for docs, SQL for metrics, web for current events); iterative refinement when the first retrieval is poor; and skipping retrieval entirely for queries that do not need it, which cuts both latency and the noise that irrelevant context injects.<br><br><strong>Common patterns:</strong> a router that classifies query type and dispatches; ReAct-style loops with retrieval as a tool; self-correction where the agent grades its own retrieved context and re-queries if it is insufficient (Self-RAG, CRAG).<br><br><strong>The costs to name honestly:</strong> multiple LLM calls per query, so latency and spend rise several-fold; non-deterministic control flow, which makes it far harder to debug and test than a fixed pipeline; and loop risk, requiring step budgets and termination conditions.<br><br><strong>How I'd decide:</strong> do not start here. Fix chunking, hybrid search, and reranking first — they are cheaper and address the majority of failures. Move to agentic RAG when your query distribution genuinely contains multi-hop or multi-source questions that no single retrieval pass can answer, and you have the evals to prove the added complexity earns its keep.` },

{ q: 'What is hybrid search, and why is it better than pure vector search?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Hybrid search combines dense vector retrieval with sparse lexical retrieval (BM25), fusing the two ranked lists — usually with Reciprocal Rank Fusion, which combines by rank position and avoids the problem that the two scoring scales are incomparable.<br><br><strong>Why pure dense search fails:</strong> embeddings are trained for semantic similarity, so they systematically underperform on exact-match needs — product SKUs, error codes, API names, acronyms, person names, version numbers, and any rare token that was not meaningfully represented in the embedder's training data. A query for <code>ERR_4021</code> against a dense index returns things that are <em>about</em> errors, not the document containing that code.<br><br><strong>Why pure sparse search fails:</strong> BM25 has no notion of synonymy or paraphrase. "How do I reset my password" misses a document titled "Credential recovery procedure."<br><br>They fail in complementary ways, which is exactly why the combination outperforms either — this is one of the most reliable wins in RAG and typically the second-highest-ROI change after reranking.<br><br><strong>Implementation notes:</strong> RRF is the sane default (no score normalization needed, one tunable constant); weighted score fusion works but requires normalizing incomparable score distributions. Retrieve a wider candidate set from both arms and let the reranker do final ordering. Many vector databases now support hybrid natively.<br><br><strong>Second-order:</strong> hybrid search adds a second index to maintain and keep in sync. That operational cost is real, but it is small relative to the recall it recovers on exactly the queries users report as broken.` },

{ q: 'What is re-ranking, and how does it improve retrieval quality?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Reranking takes the top ~50–100 candidates from fast first-stage retrieval and rescores them with a <strong>cross-encoder</strong> — a model that reads query and document <em>together</em> in one forward pass and outputs a relevance score.<br><br><strong>Why it is more accurate:</strong> a bi-encoder must compress each document into a fixed vector without ever seeing the query, so all query-document interaction is reduced to one dot product. A cross-encoder attends jointly across both, capturing term-level interaction, negation, qualifiers, and conditional relevance that a dot product cannot represent.<br><br><strong>Why you cannot use it alone:</strong> it requires a forward pass per (query, document) pair with nothing precomputable. That is fine for 100 candidates and impossible for 10 million documents — hence the two-stage architecture.<br><br><strong>Practical impact:</strong> reranking is typically the single highest-ROI addition to a naive RAG pipeline. It commonly lifts precision@5 substantially, which matters twice: better answers, and fewer chunks needed. Dropping from k=20 to k=5 cuts prompt cost and sidesteps "lost in the middle" at the same time.<br><br><strong>Cost:</strong> tens to low hundreds of milliseconds depending on model size and candidate count — a real latency addition, tunable by shrinking the candidate set or using a smaller reranker.<br><br><strong>Useful secondary benefit:</strong> the reranker score is far better calibrated than embedding cosine similarity, so it is the right signal for an abstention threshold — if the best reranked score is below your cutoff, refuse rather than answering from weak context.` },

{ q: 'How do you handle multi-document and multi-hop questions in RAG?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `Single-shot retrieval structurally cannot answer multi-hop questions. "Who is the CEO of the company that acquired Figma?" requires knowing the acquirer before you can retrieve the CEO — the second query does not exist until the first is answered, so no amount of top-k tuning helps. Recognizing that this is an architecture problem rather than a retrieval-quality problem is the key move.<br><br><strong>Query decomposition:</strong> have the model break the question into sub-questions, retrieve for each, then synthesize. Works for questions decomposable up front (comparisons, aggregations across entities).<br><br><strong>Iterative / agentic retrieval:</strong> retrieve, read, decide what is still missing, retrieve again — necessary when hop 2's query genuinely depends on hop 1's answer. This is the general solution (IRCoT, Self-Ask, ReAct-style loops).<br><br><strong>GraphRAG:</strong> build an entity-relationship graph at ingest so multi-hop becomes graph traversal rather than repeated semantic search. Expensive to build, strong when relationships are the primary access pattern.<br><br><strong>Supporting techniques:</strong> retrieve a wider candidate set and rerank aggressively; ensure metadata links chunks to their source document so you can pull sibling context; and for comparison questions, retrieve per entity separately rather than hoping one query surfaces both.<br><br><strong>The cost:</strong> every hop is another retrieval plus another LLM call, so latency and spend scale with hops, and errors compound — a wrong hop-1 answer produces a confidently wrong final answer. Cap hops, and validate intermediate results where you can.<br><br><strong>Route rather than always paying:</strong> classify whether a query is multi-hop first, and send the single-hop majority down the cheap path.` },

{ q: 'What is the "lost in the middle" problem in RAG?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Model accuracy at using retrieved context follows a U-shape by position: information at the start or end of a long prompt is used reliably, while information in the middle is often effectively ignored. The degradation is large enough that a long context of many chunks can perform <em>worse</em> than fewer, better-targeted ones.<br><br><strong>Why it matters specifically for RAG:</strong> it breaks the intuition that adding more retrieved chunks is safe. Padding the prompt with k=50 chunks "just in case" actively damages answer quality, and it means retrieval <em>ordering</em> now affects generation, not just retrieval metrics.<br><br><strong>Mitigations, in order of impact:</strong><br>• <em>Rerank and keep k small.</em> Five strong chunks beat fifty mediocre ones. This is the primary fix.<br>• <em>Order deliberately.</em> Place the highest-scoring chunks at the very beginning and very end, weakest in the middle — rather than a naive descending sort that buries rank 2 in the dead zone.<br>• <em>Compress.</em> Extract only relevant sentences from each chunk before insertion.<br>• <em>Restate the question at the end</em>, after the context, so the instruction sits in a high-attention position.<br>• <em>Decompose</em> for genuinely large inputs — process chunks separately and aggregate rather than one long pass.<br><br><strong>Measure it on your own stack:</strong> run a needle-in-a-haystack test at your real context lengths with your real model. Severity varies substantially by model and version, and a vendor's advertised context window says nothing about usable retrieval quality across it.` },

{ q: 'How do you evaluate a RAG system? Explain faithfulness, relevance, and context precision/recall.', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Evaluate the two stages separately.</strong> This is the most important structural decision — an end-to-end score tells you the system is bad, not which half to fix.<br><br><strong>Retrieval metrics</strong> (need labeled query→relevant-document pairs):<br>• <em>Context recall</em> — of all documents needed to answer, what fraction was retrieved? This is the ceiling on your system; anything not retrieved is unanswerable.<br>• <em>Context precision</em> — of what was retrieved, what fraction is relevant, and are the relevant ones ranked highly? Low precision wastes tokens and triggers lost-in-the-middle.<br>• Standard IR metrics: Recall@k, MRR, NDCG@k.<br><br><strong>Generation metrics</strong> (given the retrieved context):<br>• <em>Faithfulness / groundedness</em> — is every claim in the answer supported by the retrieved context? This is the hallucination metric, typically measured by decomposing the answer into claims and running an NLI/LLM-judge entailment check against the context.<br>• <em>Answer relevance</em> — does the answer actually address the question? A faithful answer can still be off-topic.<br>• <em>Answer correctness</em> — versus ground truth, where you have it.<br><br><strong>The diagnostic that follows:</strong> low recall → fix chunking, embeddings, hybrid search. High recall but low faithfulness → fix the generation prompt and grounding. High faithfulness but low correctness → your corpus is wrong or incomplete.<br><br><strong>Tooling and caveats:</strong> RAGAS, TruLens, and DeepEval implement these. All judge-based metrics need validation against human labels before you trust them — and never use ROUGE/BLEU for faithfulness; n-gram overlap cannot detect a fluent fabrication.` },

{ q: 'Explain Self-RAG. How does the model decide when to retrieve?', tags: [], a: `Self-RAG trains a model to emit special <strong>reflection tokens</strong> that control and critique its own retrieval, rather than always retrieving on a fixed schedule.<br><br>Four token types: <code>[Retrieve]</code> — decide whether retrieval is needed for the next segment; <code>[IsRel]</code> — is a retrieved passage relevant; <code>[IsSup]</code> — is the generated statement supported by that passage; <code>[IsUse]</code> — is the overall response useful. These are learned during fine-tuning on data annotated by a critic model, so the decisions come from the weights rather than from prompt instructions.<br><br><strong>Why on-demand retrieval matters:</strong> always retrieving is wasteful and actively harmful. For queries the model knows, or for creative and conversational turns, irrelevant retrieved context injects noise and degrades output. Self-RAG can also retrieve <em>mid-generation</em>, when a specific factual claim is about to be made, rather than only once at the start.<br><br>At inference, the reflection tokens double as a scoring signal — you can generate multiple candidate continuations and select by weighted critique scores, effectively a beam search over groundedness.<br><br><strong>The tradeoffs:</strong> it requires fine-tuning, so it is unavailable on closed APIs; the critique tokens add inference overhead; and quality depends on the critic model that generated the training annotations.<br><br><strong>The practical takeaway even if you never train it:</strong> adaptive retrieval is the right idea, and you can approximate it cheaply with a lightweight classifier or a router deciding whether a query needs retrieval at all. Compare with CRAG, which grades retrieval quality post-hoc and falls back to web search when the corpus is insufficient.` },

{ q: 'What is GraphRAG, and when would you use it over traditional RAG?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `GraphRAG builds a knowledge graph from the corpus at ingest time — an LLM extracts entities and relationships from each chunk, entities are deduplicated and merged across documents, and the graph is clustered into hierarchical communities with LLM-generated summaries per community. At query time you traverse the graph or read community summaries instead of (or alongside) doing flat semantic search.<br><br><strong>What it solves that vector RAG cannot:</strong> <em>global</em> questions — "what are the main themes across this corpus?", "how has this topic evolved?" — which no top-k retrieval can answer, because the answer is not in any five chunks; it is a property of the whole collection. It also handles multi-hop relational queries natively (traversal instead of repeated guessy semantic search) and connects facts that are stated in different documents and never co-occur in any chunk.<br><br><strong>The costs, which are substantial:</strong> indexing requires an LLM pass over every chunk for extraction, making ingest orders of magnitude more expensive than embedding — meaningful money at corpus scale. Entity resolution is error-prone and errors propagate into every downstream answer. Incremental updates are harder, since a new document can change community structure. And it adds a graph store to operate.<br><br><strong>How I'd decide:</strong> do not start here. Use vector RAG with hybrid search and reranking for the common case of local, fact-lookup questions — GraphRAG is not better at those and costs more. Adopt it when your actual query distribution is dominated by relational or corpus-level synthesis questions, or when the domain is inherently a graph (org structures, supply chains, citation networks, incident causality). A hybrid — vector retrieval for local questions, graph for global — is usually the right production shape.` },

{ q: 'How do you handle structured data (tables, SQL) in a RAG pipeline?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `The key insight: <strong>do not embed structured data and hope semantic search works.</strong> Vector similarity cannot do aggregation, filtering, or arithmetic — "what was total Q3 revenue by region" is not a retrieval question, it is a query question. Embedding a table and retrieving chunks of it produces confidently wrong numbers.<br><br><strong>For databases, use text-to-SQL:</strong> give the model the schema (plus column descriptions and a few example rows), have it generate SQL, execute against the real database, and pass the result set to the model to phrase an answer. The database does the aggregation correctly, and the model never does arithmetic.<br><br><strong>Make it safe and reliable:</strong> execute against a read-only replica with a restricted role, enforce query timeouts and row limits, validate/parse the SQL before running it, and feed execution errors back for one repair attempt. Retrieve relevant schema subsets rather than dumping a 400-table schema into context.<br><br><strong>For tables inside documents</strong> (PDFs, reports): preserve structure during parsing — never flatten a table into prose. Store the table as markdown or HTML, and index an LLM-generated <em>summary</em> of what the table contains, so semantic search can find it while the model reads the structured form. For large tables, load into a dataframe and let the model write code against it rather than reading it as text.<br><br><strong>The production shape is routing:</strong> classify each query as unstructured (vector RAG), structured (SQL), or hybrid (both, then synthesize). Getting the router right matters more than perfecting either path.` },

{ q: 'What are common RAG failure modes, and how do you debug them?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Debug in pipeline order</strong> — most teams start at the prompt and waste days, when the failure is three stages upstream.<br><br><strong>1. Parsing failures.</strong> Check the extracted text first. Broken PDF extraction, lost table structure, dropped headers — the ceiling on everything downstream. Read the actual stored chunk text for a failing query.<br><br><strong>2. Chunking failures.</strong> A claim split from its qualifier, a table cut in half, a heading orphaned from its section. Symptom: retrieved chunk is topically right but incomplete or misleading.<br><br><strong>3. Retrieval failures (most common).</strong> The needed chunk is not in the top-k. Diagnose by checking whether it is retrievable at all: search for it directly. If it never surfaces, suspect embedding-model mismatch to your domain, missing hybrid search for exact terms, or a metadata filter silently excluding it.<br><br><strong>4. Ranking failures.</strong> The right chunk is retrieved but ranked 40th. Fix with reranking.<br><br><strong>5. Generation failures.</strong> The right context is in the prompt and the answer is still wrong — the model ignored it (lost in the middle, too many chunks), overrode it with parametric knowledge, or the prompt did not instruct grounding.<br><br><strong>The instrumentation that makes this tractable:</strong> log the query, the retrieved chunk ids with scores, the assembled prompt, and the answer, for every request. Without that trace you are guessing. Then build a small labeled set of known-answerable questions and measure retrieval recall in isolation — if recall is low, no prompt change will save you.` },

{ q: 'How do you handle document updates and maintain freshness?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Track content, not files.</strong> Hash each document (and each chunk) at ingest; on re-crawl, compare hashes and only re-chunk/re-embed what actually changed. Re-embedding an unchanged corpus nightly is the default naive design and it is pure waste.<br><br><strong>Handle deletes and updates properly.</strong> An update is delete-then-insert at chunk granularity — the common bug is inserting new chunks without removing the stale ones, so the index serves both versions and the model gets contradictory context. Keep a stable <code>document_id</code> on every chunk so you can delete all chunks for a document atomically.<br><br><strong>Prefer event-driven over scheduled.</strong> Webhooks or CDC from the source system give near-real-time freshness; a nightly batch means a 24-hour staleness window. Where the source has no events, poll with change detection rather than full re-ingest.<br><br><strong>Store temporal metadata</strong> — <code>created_at</code>, <code>updated_at</code>, <code>valid_until</code>, <code>version</code> — and use it: filter out expired documents at query time, and apply recency boosting in ranking for time-sensitive corpora. Also surface the date in the context so the model can say "as of March 2026."<br><br><strong>The failure mode to design against:</strong> silent staleness. The system keeps answering confidently from a document deleted three months ago. Monitor index age distribution and alert on documents whose source has not been verified within an expected window — staleness does not throw an error, so it needs an explicit check.` },

{ q: 'How do you optimize RAG for latency in production?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>Measure the breakdown first</strong> — embed, ANN search, rerank, prompt assembly, TTFT, generation. Optimizing without that split is guesswork, and in most systems generation dominates, which means retrieval micro-optimizations buy nothing.<br><br><strong>Retrieval side:</strong> tune ANN parameters (HNSW <code>ef_search</code> is a direct recall/latency dial); run dense and sparse retrieval <em>in parallel</em>, not sequentially; cache query embeddings for repeated queries; use a smaller or quantized reranker, or shrink the candidate set from 100 to 50; and use quantized vectors (scalar/binary) with a small exact-rerank pass, which cuts both memory and search time.<br><br><strong>Generation side — where the wins are:</strong> stream the response so perceived latency collapses even when total time is unchanged; reduce k so there are fewer input tokens to prefill; use prompt caching by putting static instructions first and retrieved chunks last; cap output length; and route simple queries to a smaller model.<br><br><strong>Architectural:</strong> semantic caching of full answers for near-duplicate queries (with a conservative similarity threshold — a false cache hit is a wrong answer); precompute answers for known-common questions; and overlap work, e.g. begin reranking as first-stage results stream in.<br><br><strong>The tradeoff to state explicitly:</strong> nearly every latency optimization trades quality — lower <code>ef_search</code> costs recall, fewer chunks costs context, a smaller model costs accuracy. Run each change against your retrieval and faithfulness evals, and pick the operating point deliberately rather than discovering the regression from users.` },

{ q: 'What is the role of metadata filtering in RAG?', tags: [], a: `Metadata filtering constrains retrieval to a subset of the index using structured attributes — date range, document type, department, language, tenant, permissions — before or during vector search.<br><br><strong>Three things it does that vector similarity cannot:</strong><br>• <em>Correctness on constrained queries.</em> "Show me 2026 policies" is a filter, not a similarity question; embeddings represent topic, not date, so semantic search will happily return the 2019 policy.<br>• <em>Security.</em> Per-user access control must be a hard filter. Retrieval that returns documents a user cannot see is a data breach, and no reranking or prompt instruction can fix it after the fact.<br>• <em>Efficiency.</em> Narrowing the search space raises precision and can cut latency.<br><br><strong>Implementation matters more than it looks.</strong> <em>Post-filtering</em> (retrieve top-k, then drop non-matching) is easy but can return far fewer than k results — or zero — when the filter is selective. <em>Pre-filtering</em> restricts the candidate set first, which is correct but interacts badly with graph-based ANN indexes like HNSW, where aggressive filtering can disconnect the graph and destroy recall. Most mature vector databases implement filtered search natively with some form of adaptive strategy; know which one yours uses, because the failure mode is silently degraded recall rather than an error.<br><br><strong>Practical guidance:</strong> design the metadata schema at ingest — retrofitting fields means re-indexing. Extract what you can automatically (dates, source, type) and validate it; a filter on a field that is null for 40% of documents silently excludes them.` },

{ q: 'Compare RAG vs fine-tuning. When would you use each?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>They solve different problems, and the framing that matters is: RAG adds knowledge, fine-tuning changes behavior.</strong> Most "which should I use" debates dissolve once you classify the actual gap.<br><br><strong>Use RAG when</strong> the model lacks <em>facts</em>: private or proprietary data, information past the training cutoff, anything that changes frequently, or anything requiring citation, deletion, or per-user access control. RAG updates instantly, is auditable, and is far cheaper to iterate on.<br><br><strong>Use fine-tuning when</strong> the model lacks a <em>behavior</em>: a specific output format or style, a domain-specific task the model performs poorly at, consistent adherence to a complex policy, or when you want a small model to match a large one's performance on a narrow task (the strongest cost argument — a fine-tuned 7B replacing a frontier model at 20x lower cost).<br><br><strong>Fine-tuning is a poor way to inject facts.</strong> It requires retraining to update, provides no attribution, cannot be selectively deleted, and models trained on new facts often still hallucinate around them. Teams that fine-tune to teach the model their documentation reliably rediscover this.<br><br><strong>The practical decision path:</strong> prompt engineering first (free, instant). Then RAG (solves the majority of real gaps). Then fine-tuning, only with a clear eval showing the remaining gap is behavioral. <strong>They compose well</strong> — fine-tune a small model to follow your format and use context faithfully, then serve it with RAG. That combination often beats either alone, and beats a frontier model on cost.` },

{ q: 'What is query transformation in RAG (HyDE, decomposition, step-back)?', tags: [], a: `Query transformation rewrites the user's query before retrieval, because the raw query is often a poor retrieval key — it is phrased as a question while documents are phrased as statements, or it is a context-dependent fragment, or it is too complex for one lookup.<br><br><strong>HyDE (Hypothetical Document Embeddings):</strong> have the LLM generate a hypothetical <em>answer</em> to the query, then embed that instead of the query. It works because the fake answer lives in the same linguistic space as real documents — statements matched against statements — closing the question/answer asymmetry gap. Effective in zero-shot settings; the risk is that a hallucinated hypothetical drags retrieval off-topic on obscure queries.<br><br><strong>Query decomposition:</strong> split a compound question into independent sub-queries, retrieve for each, and merge. Necessary for comparisons and multi-part questions where one embedding cannot represent both parts.<br><br><strong>Step-back prompting:</strong> generate a more general question ("what are the principles of X?") alongside the specific one, and retrieve for both. Surfaces background context that the narrow query would miss.<br><br><strong>Multi-query / query expansion:</strong> generate several paraphrases and union the results (fused with RRF), which improves recall by covering vocabulary the user did not use.<br><br><strong>The one that matters most in practice and is often forgotten:</strong> <em>conversational query rewriting</em> — turning "what about the other one?" into a standalone query using prior turns. Without it, multi-turn RAG systems work on question one and fail on every follow-up.<br><br><strong>Cost:</strong> each transformation adds an LLM call before retrieval, so apply them selectively via a router rather than universally.` },

{ q: 'How do you implement citation and source attribution in RAG?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Assign stable ids and require the model to cite them.</strong> Label each chunk in the prompt (<code>[1]</code>, <code>[2]</code>, or a chunk id) and instruct the model to attach citations to each claim. Then <strong>verify programmatically</strong> that every cited id actually exists in the provided context — models hallucinate citation numbers, and an unverified citation is worse than none because it manufactures false confidence.<br><br><strong>Go further than id-level citation where accuracy matters:</strong> require the model to quote the supporting span verbatim, then string-match that quote against the source chunk. A quote that does not appear in the source is a detectable hallucination — this turns attribution into an automated faithfulness check rather than a UI feature.<br><br><strong>Alternative post-hoc approach:</strong> generate the answer first, then run a separate attribution pass that matches each answer sentence to supporting chunks via NLI/entailment. This often produces better prose (the model is not juggling format and content) and gives a supported/unsupported label per sentence, which you can surface or use to trigger regeneration.<br><br><strong>Map citations back to real, resolvable sources:</strong> chunk → document → URL/page/section, with enough granularity that a user can land on the exact passage. Deep links to a page number or heading anchor are the difference between a citation that builds trust and one that is decorative.<br><br><strong>Second-order:</strong> citations change user behavior — people trust cited answers more, so an incorrect citation is more damaging than an uncited wrong answer. Measure citation <em>precision</em> (does the cited source actually support the claim), not just citation presence.` },

{ q: 'How do you scale RAG to millions of documents?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>The index is the first constraint.</strong> Flat search is O(n) and stops being viable well before millions; you need ANN. HNSW gives excellent recall/latency but high memory (the graph plus full vectors in RAM); IVF-PQ trades some recall for a much smaller footprint via quantization. At tens of millions of vectors, memory is usually the binding cost, so quantization is not optional — scalar or binary quantization with a small exact-rescoring pass over the top candidates recovers most of the lost recall at a fraction of the memory.<br><br><strong>Partition rather than growing one index.</strong> Shard by tenant, domain, time period, or language, and route queries to the relevant shard(s). This cuts search cost, improves precision (fewer irrelevant near-neighbors), and makes re-indexing incremental rather than global.<br><br><strong>Retrieval quality degrades with scale, not just latency.</strong> At millions of documents there are far more plausible-but-wrong neighbors, so precision drops even at constant recall@k. Hybrid search and reranking become more important, not less — reranking a wider candidate set is how you recover precision.<br><br><strong>Ingest becomes a pipeline problem:</strong> parallelize parsing/chunking/embedding, batch embedding calls, checkpoint so a failure does not restart the run, and support incremental updates by content hash. A full re-embed of 10M chunks is a multi-day, four-figure job — design so you never need it, and pin the embedding model version accordingly.<br><br><strong>Operationally:</strong> replicas for read throughput, monitoring on recall (via a fixed labeled probe set run continuously), p99 search latency, and index memory headroom.` },

{ q: 'What is parent-child chunking, and how does it improve retrieval?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Parent-child (small-to-big) chunking embeds <em>small</em> chunks for matching but returns their <em>larger</em> parent chunk as context. You index the child vectors, and on a hit you look up and pass the parent.<br><br><strong>Why it works:</strong> it resolves the central chunking tension. Small chunks make precise embeddings — one topic per vector, so similarity is sharp and the relevant passage is actually findable. Large chunks give the model complete context to answer from. Normally you must trade one for the other; parent-child gets both, because the unit of <em>retrieval</em> is decoupled from the unit of <em>context</em>.<br><br><strong>Concretely:</strong> split a document into ~2000-token parents, split each parent into ~200-token children, embed only the children, store the parent id on each child. At query time, retrieve children, map to unique parents, deduplicate (several children often hit the same parent), and pass the parents.<br><br><strong>Variants of the same idea:</strong> <em>sentence-window retrieval</em> embeds single sentences and returns the surrounding ±k sentences — even finer matching. <em>Summary indexing</em> embeds an LLM-generated summary of a large chunk and returns the full chunk, which is useful when the raw text is poorly suited to embedding (tables, code).<br><br><strong>Watch the deduplication and budget:</strong> multiple child hits collapsing to one parent can leave you with fewer distinct sources than expected, and parents are large, so k must be smaller to respect the context budget — which is fine, since parent-child raises per-chunk quality.<br><br><strong>Practical note:</strong> this is usually a bigger win than switching to semantic chunking, and it is cheaper to implement.` },

{ q: 'Your RAG hallucinates despite having the right context. How do you fix it?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `First verify the premise — inspect the <em>fully rendered prompt</em>, not the retrieval logs. "The right context was retrieved" and "the right context reached the model" are different claims, and the gap between them is where most of these bugs live: the chunk was truncated by a token budget, ordered into the dead middle, or drowned among 40 other chunks.<br><br><strong>If the context genuinely reached the model, work through the causes in order:</strong><br>• <em>Too many chunks</em> — "lost in the middle." Rerank and cut k to 5. This alone resolves a large share of cases.<br>• <em>Parametric override</em> — the model prefers what it "knows" over the provided text, especially when the context contradicts common knowledge. Fix with an explicit instruction to answer only from context and to prefer context over prior knowledge on conflict.<br>• <em>No abstention path</em> — the context is partially relevant, so the model fills gaps rather than admitting the answer is not there. Give it an explicit refusal option with few-shot examples demonstrating abstention.<br>• <em>Sampling</em> — set temperature to 0.<br>• <em>Chunk boundaries</em> — the retrieved chunk contains a claim severed from its qualifier, so the model is faithfully reporting a misleading fragment. This looks like hallucination and is actually a chunking bug.<br><br><strong>Then add verification rather than trusting prompting:</strong> require verbatim supporting quotes and string-match them against the source; or run an NLI/judge entailment check per claim and regenerate unsupported ones. That converts hallucination from an unmeasured risk into a detected, actionable event — which is the durable fix.` },

{ q: 'Your chunk overlap causes redundant results. How do you reduce redundancy?', tags: [], a: `Overlap exists for a good reason — it prevents a claim being severed from its qualifier at a boundary — so the fix is deduplicating at retrieval time, not removing overlap.<br><br><strong>Exact and near-duplicate removal:</strong> after retrieval, drop chunks with high textual overlap. MinHash/SimHash or a simple token-Jaccard threshold handles the overlap-induced duplicates cheaply. Also dedupe by content hash at <em>ingest</em>, since real corpora contain the same document ingested from multiple sources.<br><br><strong>Diversity-aware ranking:</strong> use Maximal Marginal Relevance, which selects each next chunk to maximize relevance while penalizing similarity to already-selected chunks. The λ parameter dials relevance against diversity. This is the principled fix — it handles both overlap duplicates and genuinely redundant distinct documents.<br><br><strong>Structural fix:</strong> parent-child chunking largely dissolves the problem. Multiple overlapping children collapse to a single deduplicated parent, so redundancy is resolved by construction rather than filtered after the fact.<br><br><strong>Also consider:</strong> reducing overlap from a large fraction to 10–15%, which is usually enough for boundary safety; or splitting on structural boundaries (paragraphs, headings) where overlap matters less because the cuts are already semantically sound.<br><br><strong>Why this is worth fixing rather than tolerating:</strong> redundant chunks consume the context budget and crowd out genuinely different evidence, so the model sees the same fact three times and misses a second relevant source entirely. It degrades answers, not just efficiency.` },

{ q: 'Your retrieval is too slow with a large knowledge base. How do you speed it up?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>Profile first</strong> — split the time across query embedding, ANN search, metadata filtering, and reranking. In many "slow retrieval" reports the actual cost is reranking or an unindexed metadata filter, and tuning the ANN index would achieve nothing.<br><br><strong>ANN index tuning</strong> is the direct dial: for HNSW, lower <code>ef_search</code> trades recall for latency measurably; check that <code>M</code> and <code>ef_construction</code> were set sensibly at build time. For IVF, tune <code>nprobe</code>. Always re-measure recall when you touch these — the failure mode is silently worse answers, not an error.<br><br><strong>Quantization</strong> cuts both memory and search time: scalar (int8) quantization gives ~4x with minor recall loss; binary quantization gives ~32x with substantial loss, recovered by exact-rescoring the top candidates with full-precision vectors. At large scale this is usually the highest-leverage change, because it also lets the index fit in RAM instead of spilling.<br><br><strong>Reduce the search space:</strong> shard by tenant/domain/time and route; apply selective metadata pre-filters (ensure they are indexed).<br><br><strong>Reranking:</strong> shrink the candidate set (100→50), use a smaller cross-encoder, or batch on GPU. This is often the real bottleneck.<br><br><strong>Caching:</strong> cache query embeddings, and semantically cache full results for near-duplicate queries — with a conservative threshold, since a false cache hit returns a wrong answer.<br><br><strong>The honest framing:</strong> retrieval latency is usually a small fraction of end-to-end time versus generation. Confirm retrieval is actually your bottleneck before optimizing it — otherwise you will trade recall for a latency win users never perceive.` },

{ q: 'Your RAG returns duplicate results. How do you deduplicate?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Diagnose the source, because there are three distinct causes with different fixes.</strong><br><br><strong>1. Chunk overlap</strong> — adjacent chunks share text by design. Fix at retrieval with near-duplicate filtering, or structurally with parent-child chunking, which collapses overlapping children into one parent.<br><br><strong>2. Corpus-level duplication</strong> — the same document ingested from multiple sources, or boilerplate (headers, footers, legal notices, navigation) repeated across thousands of pages. This is the most common cause in real systems and the most damaging, because boilerplate chunks are semantically bland and match many queries. Fix at ingest: content-hash exact duplicates, and strip boilerplate during parsing.<br><br><strong>3. Genuinely distinct documents saying the same thing</strong> — multiple policies restating one rule. Not a bug, but still wasteful in context.<br><br><strong>Techniques, matched to cause:</strong> content hashing for exact duplicates at ingest; MinHash/SimHash or token-Jaccard for near-duplicates at ingest or retrieval; embedding cosine similarity above a threshold for semantic duplicates; and <strong>MMR</strong> at ranking time, which handles all three by explicitly penalizing similarity to already-selected results.<br><br><strong>Keep provenance when you dedupe.</strong> If three sources say the same thing, collapse to one chunk but retain all three citations — the corroboration is information users want, and discarding it silently weakens attribution.<br><br><strong>Why it matters:</strong> duplicates consume the context budget and crowd out different evidence. Five slots filled with one restated fact is strictly worse than five distinct facts.` },

{ q: 'Your RAG needs per-user access control. How do you implement it?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Filter at retrieval, in the index, as a hard pre-filter.</strong> This is a security boundary, so it cannot live in the prompt — instructing the model not to reveal restricted documents is not access control, and any injection or misinterpretation becomes a data breach.<br><br><strong>Implementation:</strong> store authorization metadata on every chunk at ingest — owner, ACL list, group ids, tenant id, sensitivity label — inherited from the source system. At query time, resolve the authenticated user's permissions and pass them as a mandatory filter on the vector search. The user's identity must come from your auth layer, never from anything the model produced.<br><br><strong>Use pre-filtering, not post-filtering.</strong> Post-filtering retrieves then discards, which leaks timing/count signals and can return an empty set for legitimate queries. Ensure your vector database supports filtered ANN search natively and understand its recall behavior under selective filters.<br><br><strong>Permissions change — design for it.</strong> The hard problem is not the filter, it is keeping ACLs in sync. Sync permissions from the source of truth on a short interval or via events; if a document's ACL changes and your index lags, you are serving stale authorization. Resolve group membership at query time rather than baking flattened ACLs into the index, so a revoked group membership takes effect immediately.<br><br><strong>Multi-tenancy:</strong> for strong isolation, use separate indexes or namespaces per tenant rather than a shared index with a tenant filter — it eliminates an entire class of filter-bypass bugs, at the cost of more indexes to operate.<br><br><strong>Also:</strong> caching must be keyed by permission scope, or one user's cached answer leaks to another. And log every retrieval with the user id for audit.` },

{ q: 'Your RAG fails on domain-specific jargon. How do you fix it?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `The cause is usually the embedding model: general-purpose embedders were trained on web text where your internal product names, part numbers, and specialist terms are rare or absent, so those tokens carry weak, poorly-differentiated representations.<br><br><strong>Cheapest effective fix: hybrid search.</strong> BM25 matches exact jargon tokens the dense model cannot represent — internal acronyms, error codes, SKUs, drug names. This recovers most jargon failures for near-zero effort and should be tried before anything else.<br><br><strong>Then, query-side expansion:</strong> maintain a synonym/acronym dictionary and expand queries at retrieval time ("MI" → "myocardial infarction"). Unglamorous and very effective, since it works with the embedder rather than against it.<br><br><strong>Enrich chunks at ingest:</strong> prepend document/section titles and an LLM-generated contextual summary to each chunk (contextual retrieval), which gives jargon-heavy fragments surrounding vocabulary to match against. Also index a glossary as documents in their own right.<br><br><strong>Change the model:</strong> use a domain-pretrained embedder where one exists (BioBERT-family, legal-domain models) — often a large jump for zero ongoing effort.<br><br><strong>The strongest and most expensive option: fine-tune the embedding model</strong> on your own (query, relevant-passage) pairs with hard negatives. A few thousand pairs — minable from click logs, support tickets, or LLM-generated synthetic queries over your documents — typically gives a substantial retrieval lift. The cost is that you must re-embed the entire corpus and re-run it whenever you retrain.<br><br><strong>Order of operations:</strong> hybrid search → query expansion → contextual chunk enrichment → domain embedder → fine-tuning. Measure recall at each step; stop when it is good enough.` },

{ q: 'Your text-only RAG now needs to handle images and tables. How do you extend it?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `Two architectural options, and choosing between them is the main decision.<br><br><strong>Option A — summarize into text (recommended default).</strong> At ingest, run a vision-language model over each image, chart, and table to produce a rich text description, then embed <em>that</em>. Retrieval stays purely textual, so your existing pipeline, hybrid search, and reranker all keep working. At answer time, pass the original image or the structured table alongside the summary so the model reasons over the real artifact rather than the description. This is simpler, cheaper to operate, and usually more accurate than the alternative.<br><br><strong>Option B — multimodal embeddings.</strong> Use a model like CLIP or a multimodal embedder to put images and text in one vector space, enabling direct text→image retrieval. Cleaner in principle, but these embeddings are much weaker than text embeddings for document retrieval, there is no BM25 arm to fall back on, and the tooling is less mature.<br><br><strong>Tables need their own treatment</strong> regardless: never flatten a table into prose. Preserve it as markdown/HTML, index an LLM-generated summary of what it contains for retrieval, and pass the structured form for reading. For large tables, load into a dataframe and let the model query it with code — an LLM reading a 500-row table as text will produce wrong aggregates.<br><br><strong>Parsing is the real bottleneck.</strong> Extracting figures, captions, and table structure from PDFs is where most of these projects actually fail — budget for a layout-aware parser rather than assuming extraction is solved.<br><br><strong>Keep the association:</strong> store each image/table with its caption and surrounding text, so retrieval can hit either and return both.` },

{ q: 'Your knowledge base updates frequently and needs versioning. How do you manage it?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Separate two concerns that get conflated:</strong> versioning the <em>documents</em> (which version of a policy is current) and versioning the <em>index</em> (which embedding model and chunking config produced these vectors). Both need explicit handling.<br><br><strong>Document versioning:</strong> store <code>document_id</code>, <code>version</code>, <code>valid_from</code>, <code>valid_until</code>, and a supersedes pointer on every chunk. Default retrieval filters to currently-valid versions. Keeping old versions retrievable-but-filtered rather than deleting them buys you point-in-time queries ("what did the policy say in January?") and auditability — often a compliance requirement, and cheap to support if designed in from the start.<br><br><strong>Update mechanics:</strong> treat an update as delete-all-chunks-for-document then insert, atomically. The classic bug is inserting new chunks without removing old ones, so the index serves both versions and the model receives contradictory context — which surfaces as unexplainable inconsistent answers. Use content hashing so unchanged documents are skipped entirely.<br><br><strong>Index versioning:</strong> the embedding model, chunking strategy, and preprocessing together define an index version. Changing any of them invalidates existing vectors, so pin them explicitly and store the version alongside the vectors. For a migration, build the new index in parallel (blue/green), evaluate it against your retrieval eval set, then cut over — never mutate a live index in place with mixed-model vectors.<br><br><strong>Operationally:</strong> event-driven updates via webhooks/CDC beat scheduled batch jobs for freshness; monitor index age distribution and alert on staleness, since a stale index fails silently rather than erroring.` },

{ q: 'Your RAG fails on multi-hop questions. How do you fix it?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `Recognize the structural problem first: single-shot retrieval <em>cannot</em> answer a question whose second query depends on the first answer. "Who leads the team that built X?" requires knowing the team before you can search for its lead. No top-k increase, better embedding, or reranker fixes this — it is an architecture problem, and diagnosing it as a retrieval-quality problem is the common mistake.<br><br><strong>Iterative retrieval is the general solution:</strong> retrieve, read, identify what is still missing, retrieve again (IRCoT, Self-Ask, or a ReAct loop with search as a tool). This handles genuine dependency chains because the second query is generated after seeing the first result.<br><br><strong>Query decomposition</strong> works when the sub-questions are independent — comparisons, aggregations across entities. Split, retrieve in parallel, synthesize. Cheaper and lower-latency than iteration, so route to it when applicable.<br><br><strong>GraphRAG</strong> makes multi-hop a traversal instead of repeated semantic guessing, which is the right answer when relationships are your dominant access pattern and you can afford the ingest cost.<br><br><strong>Supporting fixes:</strong> retrieve a wider candidate set and rerank hard; store document-level linkage so you can pull sibling chunks once one hop lands; and enrich chunks with entity metadata so entity-based lookup is possible.<br><br><strong>The costs:</strong> each hop is another retrieval plus another LLM call, and errors compound — a wrong hop-1 answer produces a confidently wrong final answer with no signal that anything failed. Cap hops, validate intermediate results where possible, and route: classify multi-hop up front so the single-hop majority stays on the cheap path.` },

{ q: 'Your enterprise RAG returns contradictory answers from different sources. How do you resolve conflicts?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `First distinguish the three cases, because they need different handling. <strong>Stale versus current</strong> (an outdated document was not superseded), <strong>scope difference</strong> (both correct, for different regions/products/audiences), and <strong>genuine disagreement</strong> (the corpus is actually inconsistent).<br><br><strong>Stale is the most common and is a data problem, not a model problem.</strong> Fix it with versioning: <code>valid_from</code>/<code>valid_until</code>, supersedes pointers, and a default retrieval filter to current versions. If your index serves both the 2023 and 2026 policy with no way to distinguish them, no prompting will resolve the conflict correctly.<br><br><strong>Scope differences are a metadata problem.</strong> Tag documents with applicability (region, product line, customer tier) and filter by the user's context — otherwise the model is asked to choose between two answers that are both right.<br><br><strong>For genuine disagreement, do not silently pick one.</strong> Establish a source authority hierarchy in metadata (official policy > wiki > chat export) and instruct the model to prefer higher-authority sources — but surface the conflict rather than hiding it: "Source A says X (official policy, updated Jan 2026); Source B says Y (team wiki, 2024)." Presenting the disagreement with provenance is more useful and more honest than a confident synthesis that hides it.<br><br><strong>Detect conflicts systematically:</strong> run an NLI/entailment check across retrieved chunks to flag contradictions, and route flagged cases to the conflict-presentation path or to human review.<br><br><strong>The organizational point:</strong> RAG makes existing corpus inconsistency visible. Feed detected conflicts back to document owners — the durable fix is upstream, and the retrieval system is the best contradiction detector the organization has ever had.` },

{ q: 'Your RAG returns outdated answers from an evolving knowledge base. How do you keep it current?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Ingest freshness:</strong> move from scheduled batch to event-driven updates — webhooks or CDC from the source systems — so changes propagate in minutes rather than on a nightly cycle. Where events are unavailable, poll with content-hash change detection so you only re-embed what changed.<br><br><strong>Handle deletion and supersession properly.</strong> The most common cause of stale answers is not slow ingest, it is that old chunks were never removed. Treat an update as an atomic delete-then-insert keyed on <code>document_id</code>, and verify chunk counts after — orphaned chunks from a prior version keep serving indefinitely and there is no error to alert on.<br><br><strong>Make time a first-class retrieval signal:</strong> store <code>updated_at</code> and validity windows, filter expired documents by default, and apply recency boosting in ranking for time-sensitive corpora. Include the document date in the context text so the model can qualify its answer ("as of March 2026") rather than asserting timelessly.<br><br><strong>Handle the model's own staleness:</strong> instruct it to prefer retrieved context over parametric knowledge when they conflict, since its training data is by definition older than your index.<br><br><strong>Monitor staleness explicitly.</strong> Track the age distribution of indexed documents and alert when documents exceed an expected refresh window. Staleness produces confident wrong answers, not errors — so without an explicit check it is invisible until a user reports it.<br><br><strong>For genuinely volatile data</strong> (prices, inventory, status), do not index it at all. Retrieve it live via an API tool at query time; anything that changes faster than your ingest cycle does not belong in a vector index.` },

{ q: 'Your RAG struggles with PDFs containing tables and layouts. How do you fix PDF parsing?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Accept the premise: <strong>PDF is a rendering format, not a data format.</strong> It stores glyph positions, not structure — there is no reliable notion of a table, a column, or a reading order in the file. Naive text extraction interleaves multi-column text, flattens tables into unreadable token soup, and loses headers. This is the single most common ceiling on enterprise RAG quality, and no downstream fix compensates for it.<br><br><strong>Match the tool to the PDF type.</strong> For digital-native PDFs, a layout-aware parser (PyMuPDF, pdfplumber, Unstructured, Docling) that reconstructs reading order and detects table regions from geometry. For scanned documents you need OCR, and for anything with complex layout, an ML layout-detection model (LayoutLM-family, table-transformer) that classifies regions — title, paragraph, table, figure — before extraction.<br><br><strong>The pragmatic modern answer: use a vision-language model.</strong> Render each page as an image and have a VLM transcribe it to structured markdown. It handles multi-column flow, merged table cells, and figures far better than geometric heuristics. Cost is real (one VLM call per page), but it is a one-time ingest cost against a permanent quality ceiling — usually worth it.<br><br><strong>Preserve tables as tables.</strong> Store markdown/HTML, index an LLM-generated summary of the table's contents for retrieval, and pass the structured form to the model. Never let a table become prose.<br><br><strong>Chunk on the recovered structure</strong> — sections and headings — rather than character counts, and prepend section titles to each chunk.<br><br><strong>The step teams skip:</strong> actually read the extracted text for a sample of documents before building anything on top. Most "our RAG is bad" investigations end at page one of the parser output.` },
]};
